From cde58306380b7a728ae38f5605834ae7a673882b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TWL Build <build@twl.local>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 18:46:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] verification: one address in FRONTEND_URL, whatever gets
 pasted there
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The link in the email arrived as https://www.example,https://example/verify?token=…
because the CORS list was pasted into FRONTEND_URL. Two addresses separated by a
comma is not a URL, and Gmail refused to follow it — so the first real email this
deployment ever sent was unusable.

The link builder now takes the first filled-in entry of whatever it is given, so
that class of mistake produces a working link rather than a broken one. It also
means CORS_ORIGINS remains a legitimate fallback when FRONTEND_URL is unset.

Second half, from the same report: pasting the whole link into the manual box said
"this verification link is not valid", because the box sent the URL as if it were a
token. Reasonable thing for a person to do — it is what they were sent — so the
screen now accepts either shape and pulls the token out itself.

Five tests on the address helper (91 total, no database); typecheck and production
build clean.
